Understood! You want to write the C program normally, execute it, and display its output directly on an HTML webpage — without GAE.

Use C + HTML + Apache CGI.

### 1. Install requirements

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install apache2 gcc -y
sudo a2enmod cgi
sudo systemctl restart apache2
```

### 2. Write C program

```
nano odd.c
```

```
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int n = 5;
    int odd = 2 * n - 1;

    printf("Content-Type: text/html\n\n");

    printf("<html><body>");
    printf("<h1> C Program Output </h1>");
    printf("<h2>Nth Odd Number = %d</h2>", odd);
    printf("</body></html>");

    return 0;
}
```

### 3. Compile C program

```
gcc odd.c -o odd.cgi
```

### 4. Move to CGI folder

```
sudo mv odd.cgi /usr/lib/cgi-bin/
sudo chmod +x /usr/lib/cgi-bin/odd.cgi
```

### 5. Display output in web browser

Open:

`http://localhost/cgi-bin/odd.cgi`

Browser output

# C Program Output

# Nth Odd Number = 9

Result: The C program executes on the server, and only its output is displayed in the browser. No GAE is required.
